{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>That it may go well with you and your children after you.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory derived from here the matter of the reward for commandments: if for blood, which a person's soul loathes, the one who separates from it merits reward for himself and for his children — then robbery and forbidden relations, which a person's soul desires and craves, how much more so (קל וחומר). For the Torah's prohibitions fall into three categories: the first, what nature desires, such as robbery and forbidden relations; the second, what nature loathes, such as eating blood and creeping things; and the third, what nature neither desires nor loathes, such as planting mixed species and wearing shaatnez and the like. Therefore a person who is warned against all three of them must distance and abhor in his nature the desirable category just as he distances and abhors the loathsome category, and because of the warning of the commandment he will be elevated — for if in the loathsome category reward was fixed, then in the desirable category it is all the more so (קל וחומר). And therefore our Rabbis of blessed memory said in the Midrash: A person should not say, 'I have no desire to eat pig flesh' — rather, he should say, 'I do desire it, but what can I do, for my Father in heaven decreed it upon me.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:57.694668106Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}